      question: "'Climate change is the greatest threat facing humanity in the 21st century.' Do you agree? Justify your answer. (9 marks)",
      answer: "Climate change presents profound, systemic and long-term threats. The IPCC warns that a 1.5°C rise above pre-industrial levels will cause more frequent extreme weather, sea level rise threatening 680 million coastal residents, loss of Arctic sea ice, collapse of coral reef ecosystems, and increased drought and food insecurity in already-vulnerable regions. Positive feedback loops (melting permafrost releasing methane; loss of ice reducing Earth's albedo) risk runaway warming. The threat is global and affects all aspects of human life — food, water, security, migration. However, other threats also vie for the title of 'greatest.' Nuclear war remains capable of civilisation-ending destruction. Pandemic disease (COVID-19 killed 7 million in two years) demonstrates rapid global vulnerability. Biodiversity loss — the sixth mass extinction — is partly but not entirely climate-driven. Extreme poverty currently kills more people per year than climate change. Some argue that addressing poverty and political instability first would give humanity more resilience to face climate change. In conclusion, climate change is arguably the greatest long-term systemic threat because its effects compound over time and cannot be undone quickly. However, 'greatest' depends on timescale — other threats are more immediately lethal. The most honest answer is that these threats are interconnected.",
